The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```
mutex_lock(&set->tag_list_lock);
list_for_each_entry(q, &set->tag_list, tag_set_list) {
...
}
mutex_unlock(&set->tag_list_lock);
```
and
```
mutex_lock(&set->tag_list_lock);
list_del(&q->tag_set_list);
...
blk_mq_update_tag_set_shared(set, false);
mutex_unlock(&set->tag_list_lock);
```
Reason:
The deadlock occurs because `blk_mq_quiesce_tagset()` holds `set->tag_list_lock` while calling `blk_mq_quiesce_queue_nowait()`, which does not block. However, the same lock is also held by `blk_mq_del_queue_tag_set()` when it calls `blk_mq_update_tag_set_shared()`. This function may need to wait for a queue to freeze (`blk_mq_freeze_queue_wait()`), but the queue freeze cannot complete because `blk_mq_quiesce_tagset()` is still holding the lock and preventing the timeout handler (which would unfreeze the queue) from proceeding. The result is a circular wait: thread 1 (timeout handler) holds the lock and waits for queue freeze; thread 2 (removing queue) holds the lock and waits for the timeout handler to complete.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
rcu_read_lock();
list_for_each_entry_rcu(q, &set->tag_list, tag_set_list) {
...
}
rcu_read_unlock();
```
and
```
list_del_rcu(&q->tag_set_list);
...
list_add_tail_rcu(&q->tag_set_list, &set->tag_list);
```
and removal of `INIT_LIST_HEAD(&q->tag_set_list);`.

Reason:
The fix replaces the mutex with an RCU read-side lock in `blk_mq_quiesce_tagset()` and `blk_mq_unquiesce_tagset()`, so that these functions no longer contend on `set->tag_list_lock`. This eliminates the deadlock because waiting for queue freeze (which happens inside `blk_mq_update_tag_set_shared()`) can now proceed without being blocked by the quiesce operation. The list operations in `blk_mq_add_queue_tag_set()` and `blk_mq_del_queue_tag_set()` are changed to RCU-safe versions (`list_add_tail_rcu()` and `list_del_rcu()`) to allow concurrent RCU readers. The removal of `INIT_LIST_HEAD` is necessary because the list entry may still be accessed by an RCU reader; it will be properly freed after a grace period in `blk_free_queue()`.

